Why This Is Asked
- Tests end-to-end Azure data engineering: ingestion, governance, lineage, schema drift, and disaster recovery.

Key Concepts
- Delta Lake on ADLS Gen2, Delta Live Tables, Azure Schema Registry, Purview, Event Hubs, SFTP ingestion, cross-region DR, backfill with time travel, data quality gates.

Code Example
```javascript
// High-level pseudo-setup for DLT-like pipeline (language tag as required)
const domainContract = fetchSchemaFromRegistry('telemetry');
const stream = readFromEventHub('telemetry');
const batch = readFromSFTP('/daily/transactions.csv');
const enriched = stream.union(batch).transform(/* drift-tolerant logic */);
writeDeltaLake(enriched, '/lake/telemetry');
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement cross-domain schema evolution without breaking downstream consumers?
- How would you validate lineage completeness across streaming and batch paths in Purview?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes multi-tenant isolation, CDC-style ingestion from on-prem, cross-region DR, and governance in a single Azure stack. It tests practical design decisions beyond generic patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Delta Lake schema evolution and drift tolerance
- Delta Live Tables and incremental loads
- Purview lineage and tagging
- ADLS Gen2 cross-region replication
- Tenant ACLs and per-tenant partitioning

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: enforce per-tenant schema drift tolerance during ingest
function alignSchema(incoming, targetSchema){
  const allowed = targetSchema.fields.map(f=>f.name);
  const filtered = incoming.columns.filter(c => allowed.includes(c.name));
  return ensureTypes(filtered, targetSchema);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate cross-region failover and data consistency? 
- How would you backfill for tenants with late-arriving changes while preserving idempotence?
